In a C program, you can use the `exit()` function to terminate the program. The `exit()` function causes the program to stop executing and returns a status code to the operating system. The status code can be specified as an argument to the `exit()` function. If no argument is specified, the default status code is 0.

Here's an example of how to use `exit()` to terminate a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
  int *ptr;

  ptr = (int *) malloc(10 * sizeof(int));

  if (ptr == NULL) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
  }

  // Your code here

  free(ptr);
  return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program uses `exit()` to terminate with a status code of `EXIT_FAILURE` if memory allocation fails. The `EXIT_FAILURE` constant is defined in the `stdlib.h` header file and represents a non-zero status code.

You can also terminate a C program using a signal. For example, you can use the `signal()` function to define a handler for the `SIGINT` signal (generated by the user pressing Ctrl+C). If the signal handler is executed, it can call `exit()` to terminate the program.

Here's an example of how to use a signal handler to terminate a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

volatile sig_atomic_t done = 0;

void sigint_handler(int sig) {
  done = 1;
}

int main() {
  sigset_t oldmask, newmask;
  int c;

  sigemptyset(&oldmask);
  sigfillset(&newmask);
  sigdiffset(&newmask, &oldmask);

  if (sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &newmask, &oldmask) < 0) {
    perror("sigprocmask");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
  }

  signal(SIGINT, sigint_handler);

  while (!done) {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    sleep(1);
  }

  return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program uses a signal handler to terminate the program when the user presses Ctrl+C. The signal handler sets a volatile atomic flag `done` to 1 and then returns. The main loop of the program checks the value of `done` after each iteration and exits if it is set to 1. The `exit()` function is called from the main function to terminate the program.
